    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d348">

                <pb facs="0587" n="1173" sameAs="frus:frus1949v07p2/pg1173"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890.20/7–2349: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in India</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Henderson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">New Delhi</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-07-23T11:00:00+05:30">July 23, 1949—11
                            a. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>833. Bajpai,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d348-f1"/> External Affairs, tells me that: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>Several days ago Philippine Ambassador Elizalde approached Madame
                                Pandit<anchor n="2" xml:id="d348-f2"/> telling her his government
                            instructed him inquire whether GOI<anchor n="3" xml:id="d348-f3"/> would be prepared enter
                            discussions with Philippine Govt re recent Philippine-Chinese
                            anti-Commie pact and re possibility GOI
                            associating itself with other Asian countries in coordinating endeavor
                            halt march Communism in Asia.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>
                            GOI instructed Madame Pandit inform
                            Elizalde to effect that although GOI
                            usually ready discuss any international matters with Philippine Govt, it
                            believes would be unwise so soon after announcement Philippine-Chinese
                            pact to enter discussions which might create impression that Asian
                            powers were ganging up against Soviet Union.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>When Madame Pandit gave this message Elizalde, he said he had expected
                            reply this character, in fact he had been given to understand US Govt
                            had feelings similar to that of GOI.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henderson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d348-f1" n="1" xml:id="d348-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Girja Shankar Bajpai, Secretary-General of the Indian Ministry of External
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d348-f2" n="2" xml:id="d348-f2-content">
                <p>Mme. Vijaya Lakshmi Pandit, Indian Ambassador in the United States.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d348-f3" n="3" xml:id="d348-f3-content">
                <p>Government of India.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
